## Proof

**Claim:** Yes, it is true. If $\Sigma$ is a connected surface (possibly non-compact, possibly with nonempty boundary) with $\chi(\Sigma) \geq 2$, then $\Sigma$ is homeomorphic to $S^2$.

We split into three exhaustive cases.

### Case 1: $\Sigma$ is compact with nonempty boundary

By the classification of compact surfaces, a compact connected surface with $b \geq 1$ boundary components has Euler characteristic
$$\chi(\Sigma) = 2 - 2g - b \quad \text{(orientable, genus } g\text{)} \qquad \text{or} \qquad \chi(\Sigma) = 2 - k - b \quad \text{(non-orientable, genus } k \geq 1\text{)}.$$
In both formulas, since $b \geq 1$:
$$\chi(\Sigma) \leq 2 - 1 = 1 < 2.$$
